  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Hot, dry, and smoky conditions continue through this week. \n\n- Isolated thunderstorms remain a threat for southwest Montana\n  including Butte. \n\n- No end in sight for the smoke.\n\n- Even hotter, drier, and windy conditions to end the week. \n\n- Cool down to near average temperatures next weekend, though it\n  remains dry. \n\nNot much change from the forecast yesterday. A ridge driven by the\nSouthwest Monsoon remains in place over the region through this\nweek. Central Idaho and western Montana will remain 5-10 degrees\nabove average for this time of year through Saturday. Waves of\nmonsoon moisture will move through the ridge causing some\nthunderstorms, but that will mainly be in southwest Montana. Butte\nwill be on the northwestern edge of this area of thunderstorms\nwith the best chance for thunderstorms Wednesday evening. \n\nThe smoke in the region depends on how much is produced in Oregon\neach day. While the dry weather isn't helping wildland fire\nfighters at all, at least there isn't any lightning forecast to\nstart new fires in that region. As fire suppression efforts\ncontinue in Oregon, the fires should slowly produce less smoke,\nbut that's impossible for us to predict. Smoke conditions will\nvary from day to day, but over the next week we see no reason for\na general improvement in conditions. \n\nThe ridge strengthens again late this week, causing temperatures\nof 10-15 degrees above average for Friday and Saturday. As this \nis happening, a trough from the Gulf of Alaska will move into \nwestern Canada and start to push against the ridge. This is a \nclassic break-down-of-the ridge pattern and usually involves a dry\ncold front. Current forecasts have trended slower with the cold \nfront, so the main impacts will be increased southwest winds on \nFriday and Saturday. \n\nAfter Saturday, the majority of ensembles are showing a robust\ncool down with temperatures Sunday and Monday within a few degrees\nof average for this time of year. Unfortunately, this front isn't\nbringing any moisture with it, so it will be only a minor slow\ndown of the bad fire weather conditions we're seeing this week.",
